# --- Added 2026-09-24 -------------------------------------------------------
# insulin_logs/foot_self_checks/sick_day_logs never got the 2026-08-01
# "acting for someone you support" treatment vitals_readings/symptoms
# already have (20260924071557_diabetes_self_monitoring_acting_supporter.sql).
# Found during a patient-dashboard audit: a supporter (e.g. a parent managing
# a diabetic child's account) could not log any of the three at all, and the
# app-layer code silently misattributed the write to the supporter's own
# patient_id instead -- including a real foot-problem report, whose trigger
# then raised a same-day urgent clinician_alerts row against the wrong,
# non-diabetic patient. Proves a 'manage' supporter can add all three
# (server-stamped logged_by_profile_id, never client-supplied), can never
# revise or delete one (including confirming no DELETE grant exists to
# authenticated at all), a stranger with no grant is refused, the foot-check
# alert lands on the child and never the parent, and sabotages by dropping
# the three *_insert_acting_supporter policies to confirm the insert that
# just succeeded would then fail. Extended same-day to also prove
# 20260924073141_diabetes_self_monitoring_acting_supporter_followups.sql
# (the /code-review ultra findings on that first migration): a supporter can
# read back an entry they just wrote (the missing *_select_own_entry
# policies), and setPatientReportedDiabetesType's separate RPC-level
# misattribution (a parent's diabetes-type pick for their child landing on
# the parent's own patient_diabetes_profile row instead) is fixed and a
# stranger with no grant is refused.
diabetes_self_monitoring_acting_supporter.sql

