# --- Added 2026-09-25 (second pass) ------------------------------------------
# clinical_staff_select's org-wide clause ("organisation_id = current_org_id()")
# admitted any patient in an org to a raw SELECT on every clinical_staff row --
# not just the safe name/photo/bio/credential columns the app's own queries
# select, since RLS is row-level, not column-level. Every sensitive column
# added to the table since the 20260712191500 original (indemnity_insurer,
# indemnity_policy_number, indemnity_expires_at, indemnity_exempt_by,
# staff_number, verified_by, credential_verified_by) rode along on that same
# clause. Confirmed live with a simulated patient session before the fix (see
# 20260925015430_restrict_clinical_staff_patient_read_to_safe_columns.sql):
# a patient could read another doctor's indemnity_policy_number and
# staff_number directly via the Supabase client SDK / PostgREST. Fixed by
# narrowing the base table's patient-facing read path to org staff /
# support-view-as only, and adding public.clinical_staff_directory -- a
# deliberately owner-run (not security_invoker, same exception as
# public.therapy_directory) safe-column view every patient-facing call site
# was moved to. Proves: a patient gets zero rows from the base table; the
# same patient still sees trust-display columns via the directory view; the
# directory view structurally carries no sensitive column; org staff access
# to the full base table is unaffected; and a sabotage step that reinstates
# the old broad policy inside the same rolled-back transaction reproduces the
# leak, confirming this test would have caught the original bug. Extended the
# same day (still pre-merge, found by /code-review high) with a 6th section
# proving a service-role caller (no auth.uid() at all -- cron jobs, internal
# notification routes) still sees the view's rows, per
# 20260925021440_fix_clinical_staff_directory_service_role_and_replay_guard.sql
# -- the original view's predicate resolved entirely through auth.uid()-
# dependent helpers, so a service-role caller silently got zero rows, not an
# error, breaking the quarterly-report cron's and the lab-order-request-pdf
# notification's doctor-attribution lines. Same-day review also found four
# patient-facing PostgREST embedded joins (clinical_staff!<fkey>(...) in
# patient_timeline/care_messages/second_opinion_requests/async_consults/
# verified_documents reads) that resolved against clinical_staff's own RLS,
# not this view -- fixed in application code (explicit follow-up query against
# clinical_staff_directory, merged client-side) rather than here, since
# PostgREST's "embedding through views" support couldn't be verified live in
# this environment; see the Jest regression tests next to patient-timeline.ts
# and care-messages.ts for that half of the fix.
clinical_staff_directory_column_scoping.sql

# --- Added 2026-09-25 (2) -----------------------------------------------------
# lab_providers_select and pharmacy_partners_select both still carried the
# original `for select to authenticated using (true)` shape from
# 20260705211315_care_coordination.sql -- the exact same bug class as the
# clinical_staff column-exposure fix (this file's other 2026-09-25 entry) and
# as specialist_providers before its 2026-09-10 fix
# (20260910172703_specialist_providers_commercial_columns_off_the_patient_
# surface.sql). Confirmed live before the fix: a patient could read all 4
# lab_providers rows including 4 populated cost_basis_note values (negotiated-
# rate evidence). Fixed in
# 20260925023144_restrict_lab_pharmacy_partner_read_to_safe_columns.sql:
# narrowed both base tables to admin-or-partners.*.manage, added
# public.lab_provider_directory / public.pharmacy_partner_directory (safe-
# column views, no auth.uid()-dependent predicate so no service-role carve-out
# is needed, unlike clinical_staff_directory), and repointed three PostgREST
# embedded FK-joins that would otherwise have silently gone null
# (lab-orders.ts's provider/ordered_by_staff, pharmacy-orders.ts's
# pharmacy_partner, finance/partner-settlements's lab_providers(name)) at
# follow-up queries against the new views, merged client-side. Proves: a
# patient AND an ordinary org-staff/clinician session (deliberately narrower
# than clinical_staff_directory's is_org_staff carve-out) are both refused
# either base table directly, even an is_active row; the same patient still
# sees the safe columns via both directory views; neither view structurally
# carries a commercial/internal-workflow column; admin access to the full
# base tables is unaffected; and a sabotage step that reinstates the old
# `using (true)` policy on both tables inside the same rolled-back
# transaction reproduces the leak on both, confirming this test would have
# caught the original bug. Extended the same day (still pre-merge, found by
# the same /code-review high pass) with a 7th section: both directory views
# were originally defined `where is_active`, which the old `using (true)`
# policy + plain PostgREST embed never enforced -- silently breaking
# attribution (a patient's own past lab order, a finance officer's
# historical settlement statement, a booked medication's pharmacy) the
# moment the provider/partner it referenced went inactive, and in
# pharmacy-orders.ts's usePharmacyCatalogue specifically letting a patient
# book a now-inactive pharmacy partner's medication while shown none of its
# identifying info (the location filter's "no structured address" escape
# hatch also matched a null partner). Fixed in
# 20260925024716_fix_lab_pharmacy_directory_active_filter_and_replay_guard.sql
# by dropping the `where is_active` clause from both views; the picker call
# sites that do need active-only (the finance page's new-statement lab
# picker, pharmacy-orders.ts's attachPharmacyPartners, which now drops an
# inactive-partner's medication outright rather than nulling the partner)
# filter it themselves. Section 7 proves both views keep resolving a row
# after is_active flips false, and sabotages by reinstating the buggy
# `where is_active` shape on lab_provider_directory to confirm the
# regression would have been caught. The application-level half of this fix
# (attachPharmacyPartners dropping an inactive-partner medication) has its
# own Jest regression test, apps/web/src/lib/queries/pharmacy-orders.test.ts,
# per this repo's convention of a DB proof for the DB-level defect and a
# Jest test for the application-level one.
lab_pharmacy_partner_directory_column_scoping.sql

